# ThreadSanitizer suppressions.
#
# Only for genuinely uninstrumentable third-party internals. A suppression
# here silences a real check, so each one needs a reason — and the first
# question should always be whether the first-party code can avoid the pattern
# instead. meson/matrix.sh runs the race detectors against the concurrency
# suite only (see the header), which is first-party and calls no raylib/GL
# code, so this file is expected to stay close to empty. If it grows, that is
# a signal, not a nuisance to suppress away.

# --- libc / pthread internals -------------------------------------------
# TSan understands glibc's own annotations; these only fire where a library
# uses a lock pattern TSan cannot model.
race:__resolv_conf_get
race:dl_open_worker

# --- PortAudio ----------------------------------------------------------
# The ALSA/Pulse shims publish state through their own locks without TSan
# annotations. Only reachable from the real audio device path, which the
# concurrency suite deliberately avoids.
race:Pa_*
race:pa_*

# --- raylib / GL --------------------------------------------------------
# Mesa/GLX/GLFW touch driver-global state (current context, extension
# strings) that is genuinely thread-affine but not TSan-annotated.
race:glX*
race:egl*
race:*GetProcAddress*